                      "rawText": "Workspace `run.py` was edited (real diff). Result:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\n  \"summary\": \"Round 2 replaces the round-1 four-kernel in-place chain with a CUDA-graph replay path for run(inputs) in run.py. The operator is launch-bound (baseline 15.36us = 5 tiny kernels, round-1 candidate 11.264us = 4 kernels), so this candidate collapses the whole chain into one graph launch while leaving get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference byte-identical to the baseline. Inputs are never mutated, the returned buffer is module-owned, capture is guarded and falls back to eager math on any failure.\",\n  \"diagnosis\": {\n    \"summary\": \"For an 8192-element FP32 tensor the arithmetic itself is free; p50 is pure CPU dispatch + kernel-launch cost. Round 1 already removed the redundant clone (5 kernels -> 4) and landed at 11.264us, which is still ~4 launches x ~2.8us. Shaving further kernel count in eager PyTorch is not possible for relu(a*x + b*y + c) without fusion, so the remaining lever is removing the per-call launches: record the four in-place kernels once into a CUDA graph and replay.\",\n    \"bottlenecks\": [\n      \"Per-call kernel launch overhead (4 launches dominate the 11.264us round-1 measurement)\",\n      \"Per-call CPU dispatch of four separate Tensor ops (mul/add_/sub_/relu_)\",\n      \"No eager operator fuses relu(a*x+b*y+c), so kernel count cannot drop below 4 in eager mode\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"patch\": \"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -21,7 +21,110 @@\\n def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n-def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n+# Optimized implementation.\\n+#\\n+# The operator is relu(x * 1.25 + y * 0.75 - 0.125) on a 32x256 FP32 tensor.\\n+# For 8192 elements the GPU work is negligible: p50 is dominated by per-call\\n+# CPU dispatch + kernel-launch cost.  The previous round reduced the chain to\\n+# four in-place kernels (mul/add_/sub_/relu_) and measured 11.264us, i.e. still\\n+# roughly launch bound.  This round attacks the launches themselves: the same\\n+# in-place chain is captured once into a CUDA graph for a given input signature\\n+# and afterwards replayed with a single graph launch.\\n+#\\n+# Safety properties:\\n+#   * Only the implementation path of run() changes; get_inputs /\\n+#     get_test_cases / get_benchmark_inputs / reference are untouched.\\n+#   * Inputs are never mutated (the in-place ops act on a freshly allocated\\n+#     output buffer that this module owns).\\n+#   * Replay re-reads x and y at their captured addresses, so it stays correct\\n+#     even when the caller mutates the input contents between calls.  The cache\\n+#     holds references to x and y, so those addresses cannot be recycled by a\\n+#     different tensor, and the cache key pins pointer/shape/stride/dtype/device.\\n+#   * Capture only happens on the second call with an identical signature, so a\\n+#     caller that hands us fresh tensors every call never triggers a capture.\\n+#   * Everything is wrapped: any capture failure falls back to plain eager\\n+#     execution of the same math.\\n+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n+_ALPHA_X = 1.25\\n+_ALPHA_Y = 0.75\\n+_BIAS = 0.125\\n+\\n+_GRAPH_LIMIT = 8\\n+_SEEN_LIMIT = 64\\n+_seen = {}\\n+_graphs = {}\\n+\\n+\\n+def _eager(x, y):\\n+    out = torch.mul(x, _ALPHA_X)\\n+    out.add_(y, alpha=_ALPHA_Y)\\n+    out.sub_(_BIAS)\\n+    return torch.relu_(out)\\n+\\n+\\n+def _signature(x, y):\\n+    return (\\n+        x.data_ptr(), y.data_ptr(),\\n+        tuple(x.shape), tuple(y.shape),\\n+        tuple(x.stride()), tuple(y.stride()),\\n+        x.dtype, y.dtype, x.device, y.device,\\n+    )\\n+\\n+\\n+def _fast_path_supported(x, y):\\n+    if not (x.is_cuda and y.is_cuda):\\n+        return False\\n+    if not hasattr(torch.cuda, 'CUDAGraph'):\\n+        return False\\n+    if x.dtype != torch.float32 or y.dtype != torch.float32:\\n+        return False\\n+    if x.shape != y.shape:\\n+        return False\\n+    if not (x.is_contiguous() and y.is_contiguous()):\\n+        return False\\n+    return True\\n+\\n+\\n+def _capture(x, y):\\n+    out = torch.empty_like(x)\\n+    side = torch.cuda.Stream()\\n+    side.wait_stream(torch.cuda.current_stream())\\n+    with torch.cuda.stream(side):\\n+        for _ in range(3):\\n+            torch.mul(x, _ALPHA_X, out=out)\\n+            out.add_(y, alpha=_ALPHA_Y)\\n+            out.sub_(_BIAS)\\n+            torch.relu_(out)\\n+    torch.cuda.current_stream().wait_stream(side)\\n+    graph = torch.cuda.CUDAGraph()\\n+    with torch.cuda.graph(graph):\\n+        torch.mul(x, _ALPHA_X, out=out)\\n+        out.add_(y, alpha=_ALPHA_Y)\\n+        out.sub_(_BIAS)\\n+        torch.relu_(out)\\n+    return graph, out\\n+\\n+\\n+def run(inputs):\\n+    x = inputs['x']\\n+    y = inputs['y']\\n+    if not _fast_path_supported(x, y):\\n+        return _eager(x, y)\\n+\\n+    key = _signature(x, y)\\n+    entry = _graphs.get(key)\\n+    if entry is None:\\n+        first_sighting = _seen.get(key, 0) == 0\\n+        if len(_seen) < _SEEN_LIMIT:\\n+            _seen[key] = _seen.get(key, 0) + 1\\n+        if not first_sighting and len(_graphs) < _GRAPH_LIMIT:\\n+            try:\\n+                graph, out = _capture(x, y)\\n+                entry = (graph, out, x, y)\\n+                _graphs[key] = entry\\n+            except Exception:\\n+                entry = None\\n+        if entry is None:\\n+            return _eager(x, y)\\n+\\n+    graph, out, _x, _y = entry\\n+    graph.replay()\\n+    return out\\n\",\n  \"candidates\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"candidate-02\",\n      \"title\": \"CUDA-graph replay of the fused in-place chain (single launch per call)\",\n      \"hypothesis\": \"The round-1 result (11.264us) shows the operator is launch-bound, not compute-bound: 4 kernels x ~2.8us. Eager PyTorch cannot fuse relu(a*x+b*y+c) below 4 kernels, so removing per-call launches is the only remaining lever. Capturing the identical in-place chain into a CUDA graph and replaying it reduces per-call CPU work to one graph launch, so p50 should drop toward a single-launch cost while computing bit-identical results.\",\n      \"change\": \"run(inputs) now tries a signature-keyed CUDA-graph replay path: on the second call with an identical (data_ptr, shape, stride, dtype, device) signature for x and y, the four in-place kernels (mul/add_/sub_/relu_) are captured once on a side stream into a torch.cuda.CUDAGraph and replayed thereafter on the caller's stream. First sighting, unsupported dtypes/layouts/devices, cache overflow (8 graphs), or any capture exception fall back to _eager, which is exactly the round-1 in-place chain. Inputs are never mutated; the returned tensor is the graph's module-owned output buffer. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are unchanged.\",\n      \"files\": [\"run.py\"],\n      \"sourceReferences\": [],\n      \"risks\": [\n        \"The replay path returns a module-owned buffer that is rewritten on the next replay for the same signature; a caller that stores outputs from two same-signature calls would see aliasing (correctness cases use four distinct shapes, so no clobbering there).\",\n        \"Capture requires no concurrent stream capture by the harness; any failure is caught and falls back to eager.\",\n        \"First call with a new signature is eager and one extra call is spent capturing, so the gain appears after warmup (benchmark warmup=3 absorbs this).\",\n        \"CUDA-graph replay is still a real GPU execution on the caller's stream, so event-based timing remains valid.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recommendedCandidate\": \"candidate-02\",\n  \"nextAction\": {\n    \"type\": \"candidate.plan\",\n    \"title\": \"Queue candidate-02 on the shared GPU for 4/4 correctness + full benchmark\",\n    "
                    },
